```{r, include = FALSE}
knitr::opts_chunk$set(collapse = TRUE, comment = "#>")
options(condepe.quiet = TRUE, tibble.print_min = 6)
```

The **Observatório de Pernambuco** is the statistical database of
Condepe/Fidem, the planning and research agency of the State of Pernambuco,
Brazil. It gathers series from IBGE, the ministries of health, education,
labour and finance, the electoral court, state agencies and others, for the
185 municipalities of Pernambuco and the State as a whole. Every source is
published in the **same long format**, so a handful of functions covers all
of them.

```{r setup}
library(condepe)
```

## What is there

`condepe_catalog()` lists the sources: one row each, with the axis and theme
it belongs to, who originally publishes it, how many rows, indicators and
localities it has, and the years it covers.

```{r}
cat <- condepe_catalog()
cat[, c("id_fonte", "tema", "indicadores", "ano_inicio", "ano_fim")]
```

The catalogue is versioned; the version and the citation to use travel with
it:

```{r}
attr(cat, "version")
attr(cat, "citation")
```

To find a source by subject, filter the catalogue like any data frame:

```{r}
cat[cat$tema == "Saúde", c("id_fonte", "descricao")]
```

## The indicators of a source

```{r}
condepe_indicators("datasus_mortalidade_infantil")
```

```{r}
ind <- condepe_indicators("sidra_populacao_estimada")
ind
```

## A series

`condepe_series()` filters a source by indicator and, optionally, by IBGE
code (7 digits for municipalities, `"26"` for the State) and by year. The
population of Recife (2611606):

```{r}
recife <- condepe_series(
  "sidra_populacao_estimada", "Populacao residente estimada",
  code = "2611606", years = 2015:2026
)
recife[, c("nm_municipio", "ano", "valor", "unidade")]
```

Look up codes with `condepe_localities()`:

```{r}
loc <- condepe_localities()
loc[grepl("Petrolina|Caruaru", loc$nm_municipio), ]
```

## Monthly series

Monthly sources carry the period twice: in `mes`, and in the full label
`indicador` (`"... [MM/YYYY]"`). Filter by `indicador_base`, the label
without the period:

```{r}
suape <- condepe_series(
  "porto_suape_carga", "Porto de Suape - Carga bruta total",
  years = 2026
)
suape[, c("ano", "mes", "indicador", "valor", "unidade")]
```

## Whole sources and the columns

`condepe_data()` returns a source in full. The 15 columns keep the
Portuguese names under which the Observatory publishes them, so a result can
always be traced back to the official files:

```{r}
nasc <- condepe_data("datasus_sinasc")
names(nasc)
```

| Column | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `cd_municipio_ibge`, `nm_municipio` | IBGE code (7 digits; `"26"` is the State) and name |
| `ano`, `mes` | year; month for monthly series (`NA` otherwise) |
| `indicador`, `indicador_base` | full label (with `[MM/YYYY]` when monthly) and label without the period |
| `valor`, `unidade`, `natureza` | value, unit, `estoque` (stock) or `fluxo` (flow) |
| `id_fonte`, `eixo`, `tema`, `assunto` | source and its place in the catalogue |
| `fonte_original`, `arquivo_origem` | original publisher and the file or API call the value came from |

A missing value never becomes zero: what the original source does not publish
is simply absent.

## Cache, mirror and failures

Files are downloaded once per catalogue version and kept in
`condepe_cache_dir()`, checked against the SHA-256 published in the
catalogue; when a new version comes out, the old files are dropped. The
catalogue itself is trusted for six hours (`refresh = TRUE` asks again).

Data come from the canonical endpoint,
<https://cf.pe.gov.br/observatorio/dados/atual/>. If it does not answer, the
package reads the same files from the Hugging Face mirror
[`mlkwy/observatorio-pernambuco`](https://huggingface.co/datasets/mlkwy/observatorio-pernambuco).
If neither answers, functions warn and return zero-row tibbles with the
documented columns, so scripts degrade instead of breaking.

## Citation and license

The data are published under [CC BY 4.0](https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/):
cite Condepe/Fidem and the version (`attr(condepe_catalog(), "citation")`).
